  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH NEXT SUNDAY/... \nModels continue to suggest a more organized large scale troughing\npattern setting up later this week with the parent low setting up\nalong the British Colombia coast before diving southward into the\nPacific Northwest by Thursday. A secondary shortwave or weak\nclosed low may also form west of California Wednesday into\nThursday, more associated with the sub-tropical jet. What is \nlikely to be more important is the eventual phasing of the polar \njet over the Pacific Northwest with the stronger subtropical jet \nby next Friday. Both the EPS and GEFS support this phasing, while \nalso strengthening the subtropical jet to near record strength \nfor this time of year just west of Baja on Friday. Guidance then \nsupports a fairly strong weather system (with H5 heights within \nthe 10th percentile of climatology) developing within the base of\nthe trough by next weekend. Although we are at around 5-7 days \nout from this next expected weather event, there is enough model \nevidence for us to begin worrying about another significant \nprecipitation event. A bit more than 50% of the ensemble members \nfavor the trough deepening just off the West Coast Friday into \nnext weekend, likely pushing a modest to strong atmospheric river \n(AR) somewhere into the Southwestern U.S. next weekend.\n\nAs the trough first begins to set up during the middle part of the\nweek, there is high confidence in strong northward moisture \nadvection in the upper levels out of Mexico Wednesday night into\nThursday. This should be followed by lower level moisture \nadvection later Thursday into Friday. Although the timing may\nchange and assuming the positioning of the subtropical jet is\ncorrect, shower chances may begin as early as late Thursday and \ncarry into Friday under broad divergence aloft and modest upslope \nsoutherly flow. This first period of rain chances very well could \nchange and confidence is still quite low in how much rain could \nfall.\n\nScattered mostly high terrain rain chances may continue into \nSaturday before the main weather system traverses the region at \nsome point Saturday night and Sunday. Additional moisture \nadvection is likely to occur, associated with the AR, right ahead\nof the weekend system, but it is too early to know where exactly \nthe best moisture and forcing will end up. Even if we do not get a\ndirect hit, ensemble guidance is heavily favoring at least a \npartial hit from the AR late Saturday into Sunday. Preliminary QPF\nnumbers show upwards of an inch may be possible over the south- \ncentral Arizona lower deserts to upwards of 2\" possible in the \nhigh terrain north and east of Phoenix. These numbers very well \ncould change and seem to lean more on the optimistic side. If the \nupper end of precip guidance comes to fruition, locations across \ncentral Arizona are expected to have to worry about rising stream\nand river levels again, but it is doubtful there will be big \nflood concern due to the longer duration of the event. Given we \nare still outside the winter season, this weather system will be \non the warmer side with snow levels likely staying above 9-10K \nfeet.",
